    {
      "math_academy_id": "q-344842",
      "knowledge_point_id": "48e49ade-8bfe-56e7-8b52-950fa84e4bb0",
      "knowledge_point": "Finding Critical Points of Exponential Functions",
      "problem": "In **ascending order**, the critical points of the function $f(x)={e}^{{x}^{3}-15{x}^{2}/2+12x}$ are\n\n$x=$\n{{field-1}}\n$\\,$ and $\\,x=$\n{{field-2}}.",
      "worked_solution": "A critical point of $f(x)$ is a point $x=c$ in the domain of $f$ such that either ${f}^{′}(c)=0$ or ${f}^{′}(c)$ does not exist.\n\nDifferentiating $f(x),$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({e}^{{x}^{3}-15{x}^{2}/2+12x}) \\\\  & ={({x}^{3}-\\frac{15}{2}{x}^{2}+12x)}^{′}{e}^{{x}^{3}-15{x}^{2}/2+12x} \\\\  & =(3{x}^{2}-15x+12){e}^{{x}^{3}-15{x}^{2}/2+12x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now proceed to find the critical points:\n\n- First, we compute the points where ${f}^{′}(c)=0.$ We set the derivative equal to zero, and solve for $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(3{x}^{2}-15x+12){e}^{{x}^{3}-15{x}^{2}/2+12x} & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince ${e}^{{x}^{3}-15{x}^{2}/2+12x}\\ne 0$ for all $x,$ we can divide this equation by ${e}^{{x}^{3}-15{x}^{2}/2+12x}$ and solve the resulting equation.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3{x}^{2}-15x+12 & =0 \\\\ 3({x}^{2}-5x+4) & =0 \\\\ 3(x-1)(x-4) & =0 \\\\ x & =1,\\,4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $f(x)$ has stationary points at $x=1$ and $x=4.$\n\n- Second, we note that ${f}^{′}(x)$ exists for all values of $x$ in the domain of $f(x).$ So $f(x)$ has no non-stationary critical points.\n\nTherefore, in ascending order, the critical points of $f(x)$ are  $x=1$ and  $x=4.$",
